---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout 
  title="What is an Amortization Schedule and How Does It Work? | bestfinancecalculator.com"
  description="Understand what an amortization schedule is, how it works, and why it matters for your mortgage or loan. Includes a real example and free calculator."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <!-- Back to Blog Link -->
    <div class="mb-8">
      <a href="/blog" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm font-semibold text-brand hover:text-brand-hover transition-colors">
        <svg class="h-4 w-4" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-width="2" stroke="currentColor">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M10.5 19.5L3 12m0 0l7.5-7.5M3 12h18" />
        </svg>
        Back to Blog
      </a>
    </div>

    <!-- Article Header -->
    <header class="mb-12">
      <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full bg-brand/10 px-3 py-1 text-xs font-semibold text-brand ring-1 ring-inset ring-brand/20">
        Loan Education
      </span>
      <h1 class="mt-4 text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold font-display tracking-tight text-main-text leading-tight">
        What is an Amortization Schedule and How Does It Work?
      </h1>
      <div class="mt-6 flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-muted-text border-b border-border pb-8">
        <span>Published: June 10, 2026</span>
        <span class="h-1 w-1 rounded-full bg-border"></span>
        <span>4 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Article Content -->
    <article class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none text-muted-text space-y-8 leading-relaxed">
      <p class="text-lg text-main-text/80">
        An amortization schedule is a complete table showing every payment you will make over the life of your loan, broken down into how much goes toward interest and how much reduces your principal balance. Understanding it can change how you think about your mortgage.
      </p>

      <!-- Section: Why Your Early Payments Are Mostly Interest -->
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-main-text">
          Why Your Early Payments Are Mostly Interest
        </h2>
        <p>
          This surprises most homeowners. In the first year of a 30-year mortgage, the majority of every payment goes to interest, not reducing your loan balance. On a $320,000 loan at 6.5%, your first payment of $2,022 breaks down as roughly $1,733 in interest and only $289 in principal. By year 25, that same payment is $1,100 principal and $922 interest.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section: How Amortization Works -->
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-main-text">
          How Amortization Works
        </h2>
        <p>
          Each month, interest is calculated on your remaining balance. Because your balance decreases slightly each month, the interest portion also decreases slightly, and the principal portion increases. This gradual shift is called amortization.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section: Real Example Over 30 Years -->
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-main-text">
          Real Example Over 30 Years
        </h2>
        <p>
          Let's trace how a $320,000 loan at 6.5% interest develops over its term:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Year 1:</strong> You pay $24,264 total; $20,733 goes to interest, and only $3,531 reduces your balance.</li>
          <li><strong>Year 10:</strong> Your balance is down to $285,000.</li>
          <li><strong>Year 20:</strong> Your balance is down to $218,000.</li>
          <li><strong>Year 30:</strong> The balance finally reaches zero.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- Section: Why This Matters for Your Financial Decisions -->
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-main-text">
          Why This Matters for Your Financial Decisions
        </h2>
        <p>
          Knowing your amortization schedule helps you decide whether to make extra payments, refinance, or sell. If you sell after 5 years without extra payments, you have barely reduced your principal. Many homeowners are surprised how little equity they have built in the early years.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section: How Extra Payments Change Everything -->
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-main-text">
          How Extra Payments Change Everything
        </h2>
        <p>
          Making one extra payment per year on a 30-year mortgage can cut your loan term by 4 to 5 years and save tens of thousands in interest. The amortization schedule shows you exactly where that money goes.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section: View Your Full Schedule -->
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-display text-main-text">
          View Your Full Schedule
        </h2>
        <p>
          Our free mortgage calculator generates a complete year-by-year amortization schedule instantly. See exactly how much principal and interest you pay each year and how your balance reduces over time. No email required, no signup, works in your browser.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Call to Action Box -->
    <div class="mt-12 rounded-md border border-border bg-surface p-8 text-center space-y-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold font-display text-main-text">
        Ready to see your amortization table?
      </h3>
      <p class="text-muted-text max-w-lg mx-auto text-sm">
        Input your loan parameters and expand the "View Full Amortization Schedule" section at the bottom of the page to trace your yearly balance.
      </p>
      <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
        <a 
          href="/mortgage-calculator" 
          class="inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-md bg-brand hover:bg-brand-hover px-6 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-white shadow-sm transition-colors duration-200"
        >
          Mortgage Calculator
        </a>
        <a 
          href="/loan-calculator" 
          class="inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-md border border-border bg-page hover:bg-surface px-6 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-main-text shadow-sm transition-colors duration-200"
        >
          Personal Loan Calculator
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
